    ('ai', 370, '{"title":"Artificial Intelligence Foundations","tagline":"How machines search, reason, learn, and understand language, built from scratch in Python.","level":"Beginner → Intermediate (basic Python)","hours":40,"accent":"#A855F7","badge":"AI","requires":["python"],"category":"ai","stack":"Python","tutorGuidelines":["Before importing a library (numpy, pandas, scikit-learn, torch, etc.), check it is installed with python3 -c \"import <name>; print(<name>.__version__)\". If it is missing, ask the learner before installing, and prefer a virtual environment (python3 -m venv .venv).","ML library APIs change between versions. Check the installed version and use APIs that exist in it; if unsure whether a function or argument exists, test it in the terminal first.","Never make up accuracy scores, loss values, or training results — run the code and report the real numbers. Explain that results vary with random seeds, and set seeds when reproducibility matters.","Do not invent facts about specific AI models, companies, release dates, parameter counts, or benchmark leaderboards. Your knowledge may be outdated — say so and teach the underlying concepts instead.","Keep examples small enough to run quickly on a laptop CPU (small datasets such as the ones built into scikit-learn, few epochs). Say when a real project would need more data or a GPU.","Be honest about limitations: models can be wrong, biased, or overfit. Teach evaluation on held-out data and never present a model output as guaranteed truth.","Build small versions from scratch first (a search algorithm, naive Bayes, a single neuron) before showing the library equivalent, so the learner sees there is no magic.","Keep the math intuitive and visual. Introduce a formula only after the idea is clear, and show it as working NumPy code."],"buildsOn":"python","modules":[{"id":"what-ai-really-is","title":"What AI Really Is","description":"Cut through the hype: what AI is, what it is not, and how the field fits together.","lessons":[{"id":"defining-ai-intelligent-agents-and-rational-behavior","title":"Defining AI: intelligent agents and rational behavior"},{"id":"narrow-ai-vs-general-ai","title":"Narrow AI vs general AI"},{"id":"a-short-history-symbolic-ai-machine-learning-deep-learning","title":"A short history: symbolic AI, machine learning, deep learning"},{"id":"how-ai-machine-learning-deep-learning-and-data-science-relate","title":"How AI, machine learning, deep learning, and data science relate"},{"id":"where-ai-is-used-today-and-where-it-fails","title":"Where AI is used today, and where it fails"},{"id":"setting-up-python-a-virtual-environment-and-numpy","title":"Setting up Python, a virtual environment, and NumPy"}]},{"id":"the-math-you-actually-need","title":"The Math You Actually Need","description":"Just enough linear algebra, calculus, and probability, taught through code.","lessons":[{"id":"vectors-and-matrices-with-numpy","title":"Vectors and matrices with NumPy"},{"id":"dot-products-and-what-they-measure","title":"Dot products and what they measure"},{"id":"derivatives-and-gradients-as-which-way-is-downhill","title":"Derivatives and gradients as \"which way is downhill\""},{"id":"probability-basics-events-conditional-probability","title":"Probability basics: events, conditional probability"},{"id":"bayes-theorem-with-a-worked-example","title":"Bayes'' theorem with a worked example"},{"id":"mean-variance-and-the-normal-distribution","title":"Mean, variance, and the normal distribution"},{"id":"exercise-implement-vector-and-probability-helpers","title":"Exercise: implement vector and probability helpers"}]},{"id":"problem-solving-as-search","title":"Problem Solving as Search","description":"Classic AI: agents that find their way through a space of possibilities.","lessons":[{"id":"agents-environments-states-and-actions","title":"Agents, environments, states, and actions"},{"id":"modeling-a-problem-as-a-state-space","title":"Modeling a problem as a state space"},{"id":"breadth-first-search","title":"Breadth-first search"},{"id":"depth-first-search","title":"Depth-first search"},{"id":"uniform-cost-search","title":"Uniform-cost search"},{"id":"heuristics-and-a-search","title":"Heuristics and A* search"},{"id":"exercise-a-maze-solving-agent","title":"Exercise: a maze-solving agent"}]},{"id":"games-and-adversarial-search","title":"Games & Adversarial Search","description":"Teach a program to play against you and plan ahead.","lessons":[{"id":"game-trees-and-turn-based-games","title":"Game trees and turn-based games"},{"id":"the-minimax-algorithm","title":"The minimax algorithm"},{"id":"alpha-beta-pruning","title":"Alpha-beta pruning"},{"id":"evaluation-functions-for-large-games","title":"Evaluation functions for large games"},{"id":"exercise-an-unbeatable-tic-tac-toe-ai","title":"Exercise: an unbeatable tic-tac-toe AI"}]},{"id":"knowledge-and-reasoning-under-uncertainty","title":"Knowledge & Reasoning Under Uncertainty","description":"Represent what an agent knows, and reason when the world is uncertain.","lessons":[{"id":"propositional-logic-and-knowledge-bases","title":"Propositional logic and knowledge bases"},{"id":"inference-deriving-new-facts-from-rules","title":"Inference: deriving new facts from rules"},{"id":"rule-based-expert-systems-and-their-limits","title":"Rule-based expert systems and their limits"},{"id":"reasoning-with-probability","title":"Reasoning with probability"},{"id":"bayesian-networks-the-intuition","title":"Bayesian networks: the intuition"},{"id":"exercise-a-naive-bayes-spam-filter-from-scratch","title":"Exercise: a naive Bayes spam filter from scratch"}]},{"id":"machine-learning-fundamentals","title":"Machine Learning Fundamentals","description":"Instead of writing rules, let the program learn them from data.","lessons":[{"id":"supervised-unsupervised-and-reinforcement-learning","title":"Supervised, unsupervised, and reinforcement learning"},{"id":"features-labels-and-datasets","title":"Features, labels, and datasets"},{"id":"training-and-test-sets-why-we-hold-data-back","title":"Training and test sets: why we hold data back"},{"id":"linear-regression","title":"Linear regression"},{"id":"classification-with-logistic-regression","title":"Classification with logistic regression"},{"id":"k-nearest-neighbors","title":"k-nearest neighbors"},{"id":"decision-trees","title":"Decision trees"},{"id":"evaluation-metrics-accuracy-precision-recall","title":"Evaluation metrics: accuracy, precision, recall"},{"id":"overfitting-and-underfitting","title":"Overfitting and underfitting"},{"id":"exercise-train-and-evaluate-a-classifier-with-scikit-learn","title":"Exercise: train and evaluate a classifier with scikit-learn"}]},{"id":"neural-networks","title":"Neural Networks","description":"From a single artificial neuron to a network that learns.","lessons":[{"id":"the-perceptron","title":"The perceptron"},{"id":"building-a-neuron-from-scratch-in-numpy","title":"Building a neuron from scratch in NumPy"},{"id":"activation-functions-sigmoid-tanh-relu","title":"Activation functions: sigmoid, tanh, ReLU"},{"id":"layers-and-the-forward-pass","title":"Layers and the forward pass"},{"id":"loss-functions","title":"Loss functions"},{"id":"gradient-descent","title":"Gradient descent"},{"id":"backpropagation-the-intuition","title":"Backpropagation: the intuition"},{"id":"exercise-train-a-tiny-network-to-learn-xor","title":"Exercise: train a tiny network to learn XOR"},{"id":"a-first-look-at-pytorch-tensors","title":"A first look at PyTorch tensors"}]},{"id":"deep-learning-architectures","title":"Deep Learning Architectures","description":"The ideas behind modern vision and language models.","lessons":[{"id":"why-deeper-networks-help-and-what-makes-them-hard-to-train","title":"Why deeper networks help, and what makes them hard to train"},{"id":"convolutional-neural-networks-for-images","title":"Convolutional neural networks for images"},{"id":"recurrent-networks-for-sequences","title":"Recurrent networks for sequences"},{"id":"the-attention-mechanism","title":"The attention mechanism"},{"id":"transformers-at-a-high-level","title":"Transformers at a high level"},{"id":"transfer-learning-and-pretrained-models","title":"Transfer learning and pretrained models"}]},{"id":"natural-language-processing-and-llms","title":"Natural Language Processing & LLMs","description":"How computers work with text, up to today''s large language models.","lessons":[{"id":"text-preprocessing-and-tokenization","title":"Text preprocessing and tokenization"},{"id":"bag-of-words-and-tf-idf","title":"Bag of words and TF-IDF"},{"id":"word-embeddings-meaning-as-vectors","title":"Word embeddings: meaning as vectors"},{"id":"language-models-predicting-the-next-token","title":"Language models: predicting the next token"},{"id":"how-llms-are-trained-pretraining-fine-tuning-human-feedback","title":"How LLMs are trained: pretraining, fine-tuning, human feedback"},{"id":"prompting-an-llm-effectively","title":"Prompting an LLM effectively"},{"id":"why-llms-hallucinate-and-how-to-reduce-it","title":"Why LLMs hallucinate, and how to reduce it"},{"id":"calling-an-llm-api-safely-keys-in-environment-variables","title":"Calling an LLM API safely (keys in environment variables)"}]},{"id":"reinforcement-learning","title":"Reinforcement Learning","description":"Agents that learn by trial, error, and reward.","lessons":[{"id":"rewards-states-actions-and-policies","title":"Rewards, states, actions, and policies"},{"id":"exploration-vs-exploitation","title":"Exploration vs exploitation"},{"id":"multi-armed-bandits","title":"Multi-armed bandits"},{"id":"markov-decision-processes","title":"Markov decision processes"},{"id":"q-learning","title":"Q-learning"},{"id":"exercise-a-q-learning-agent-in-a-grid-world","title":"Exercise: a Q-learning agent in a grid world"}]},{"id":"ai-ethics-safety-and-responsibility","title":"AI Ethics, Safety & Responsibility","description":"Build AI that is fair, honest about its limits, and safe to deploy.","lessons":[{"id":"bias-in-data-and-models","title":"Bias in data and models"},{"id":"fairness-measuring-and-mitigating-harm","title":"Fairness: measuring and mitigating harm"},{"id":"privacy-and-responsible-data-use","title":"Privacy and responsible data use"},{"id":"explainability-why-did-the-model-decide-that","title":"Explainability: why did the model decide that?"},{"id":"ai-safety-and-misuse","title":"AI safety and misuse"},{"id":"a-responsible-deployment-checklist","title":"A responsible deployment checklist"}]},{"id":"capstone-project","title":"Capstone Project","description":"Take an AI project from idea to honest, evaluated result.","lessons":[{"id":"choosing-a-problem-and-a-dataset","title":"Choosing a problem and a dataset"},{"id":"building-a-simple-baseline-first","title":"Building a simple baseline first"},{"id":"training-and-evaluating-a-model","title":"Training and evaluating a model"},{"id":"error-analysis-learning-from-mistakes","title":"Error analysis: learning from mistakes"},{"id":"improving-the-model-step-by-step","title":"Improving the model step by step"},{"id":"presenting-results-and-limitations-honestly","title":"Presenting results and limitations honestly"}]}]}'::jsonb),
    ('machine-learning', 380, '{"title":"Machine Learning with scikit-learn","tagline":"The practical ML workflow: clean data, train models, and evaluate them honestly.","level":"Intermediate (Python required)","hours":35,"accent":"#F7931E","badge":"ML","requires":["python"],"category":"ai","stack":"Python","tutorGuidelines":["Before importing a library (numpy, pandas, scikit-learn, torch, etc.), check it is installed with python3 -c \"import <name>; print(<name>.__version__)\". If it is missing, ask the learner before installing, and prefer a virtual environment (python3 -m venv .venv).","ML library APIs change between versions. Check the installed version and use APIs that exist in it; if unsure whether a function or argument exists, test it in the terminal first.","Never make up accuracy scores, loss values, or training results — run the code and report the real numbers. Explain that results vary with random seeds, and set seeds when reproducibility matters.","Do not invent facts about specific AI models, companies, release dates, parameter counts, or benchmark leaderboards. Your knowledge may be outdated — say so and teach the underlying concepts instead.","Keep examples small enough to run quickly on a laptop CPU (small datasets such as the ones built into scikit-learn, few epochs). Say when a real project would need more data or a GPU.","Be honest about limitations: models can be wrong, biased, or overfit. Teach evaluation on held-out data and never present a model output as guaranteed truth.","Prefer datasets that ship with scikit-learn (sklearn.datasets) so examples work offline. Say so before downloading anything.","Always compare a model against a simple baseline (e.g. DummyClassifier) and warn about data leakage whenever preprocessing touches the test set."],"buildsOn":"python","modules":[{"id":"the-ml-workflow","title":"The ML Workflow","description":"What a real machine learning project looks like from start to finish.","lessons":[{"id":"what-machine-learning-is-and-when-not-to-use-it","title":"What machine learning is (and when not to use it)"},{"id":"the-end-to-end-workflow-problem-data-model-evaluation","title":"The end-to-end workflow: problem, data, model, evaluation"},{"id":"setting-up-a-virtual-environment-with-numpy-pandas-scikit-learn-matplotlib","title":"Setting up a virtual environment with NumPy, pandas, scikit-learn, matplotlib"},{"id":"your-first-model-in-10-lines","title":"Your first model in 10 lines"},{"id":"the-scikit-learn-api-fit-predict-transform","title":"The scikit-learn API: fit, predict, transform"}]},{"id":"working-with-data","title":"Working with Data","description":"Load, explore, and understand data before modeling it.","lessons":[{"id":"numpy-arrays-for-ml","title":"NumPy arrays for ML"},{"id":"loading-data-with-pandas","title":"Loading data with pandas"},{"id":"exploring-a-dataset-shape-types-summary-statistics","title":"Exploring a dataset: shape, types, summary statistics"},{"id":"visualizing-distributions-and-relationships-with-matplotlib","title":"Visualizing distributions and relationships with matplotlib"},{"id":"correlation-and-what-it-does-not-tell-you","title":"Correlation and what it does not tell you"},{"id":"exercise-explore-a-real-dataset","title":"Exercise: explore a real dataset"}]},{"id":"data-preprocessing","title":"Data Preprocessing","description":"Turn messy real-world data into something a model can learn from.","lessons":[{"id":"handling-missing-values","title":"Handling missing values"},{"id":"encoding-categorical-features","title":"Encoding categorical features"},{"id":"feature-scaling-standardization-and-normalization","title":"Feature scaling: standardization and normalization"},{"id":"train-test-split-and-stratification","title":"Train/test split and stratification"},{"id":"data-leakage-and-how-to-avoid-it","title":"Data leakage and how to avoid it"},{"id":"pipelines-and-columntransformer","title":"Pipelines and ColumnTransformer"},{"id":"feature-engineering-basics","title":"Feature engineering basics"}]},{"id":"regression","title":"Regression","description":"Predict numbers: prices, temperatures, demand.","lessons":[{"id":"linear-regression","title":"Linear regression"},{"id":"polynomial-features","title":"Polynomial features"},{"id":"regularization-ridge-and-lasso","title":"Regularization: Ridge and Lasso"},{"id":"regression-metrics-mae-mse-rmse-r2","title":"Regression metrics: MAE, MSE, RMSE, R²"},{"id":"residual-plots","title":"Residual plots"},{"id":"exercise-predict-house-prices","title":"Exercise: predict house prices"}]},{"id":"classification","title":"Classification","description":"Predict categories: spam or not, disease or healthy.","lessons":[{"id":"logistic-regression","title":"Logistic regression"},{"id":"k-nearest-neighbors","title":"k-nearest neighbors"},{"id":"support-vector-machines","title":"Support vector machines"},{"id":"decision-trees","title":"Decision trees"},{"id":"the-confusion-matrix","title":"The confusion matrix"},{"id":"precision-recall-and-f1","title":"Precision, recall, and F1"},{"id":"roc-curves-and-auc","title":"ROC curves and AUC"},{"id":"imbalanced-classes","title":"Imbalanced classes"},{"id":"exercise-build-a-classifier-and-pick-the-right-metric","title":"Exercise: build a classifier and pick the right metric"}]},{"id":"ensemble-methods","title":"Ensemble Methods","description":"Combine many weak models into a strong one.","lessons":[{"id":"bagging-and-random-forests","title":"Bagging and random forests"},{"id":"gradient-boosting","title":"Gradient boosting"},{"id":"feature-importance-and-its-pitfalls","title":"Feature importance and its pitfalls"},{"id":"exercise-beat-your-earlier-model-with-an-ensemble","title":"Exercise: beat your earlier model with an ensemble"}]},{"id":"unsupervised-learning","title":"Unsupervised Learning","description":"Find structure in data without labels.","lessons":[{"id":"k-means-clustering","title":"k-means clustering"},{"id":"choosing-the-number-of-clusters","title":"Choosing the number of clusters"},{"id":"hierarchical-clustering","title":"Hierarchical clustering"},{"id":"dimensionality-reduction-with-pca","title":"Dimensionality reduction with PCA"},{"id":"anomaly-detection","title":"Anomaly detection"},{"id":"exercise-customer-segmentation","title":"Exercise: customer segmentation"}]},{"id":"model-selection-and-tuning","title":"Model Selection & Tuning","description":"Choose and tune models without fooling yourself.","lessons":[{"id":"cross-validation","title":"Cross-validation"},{"id":"the-bias-variance-trade-off","title":"The bias-variance trade-off"},{"id":"learning-curves-and-validation-curves","title":"Learning curves and validation curves"},{"id":"hyperparameter-tuning-with-grid-and-random-search","title":"Hyperparameter tuning with grid and random search"},{"id":"keeping-a-final-untouched-test-set","title":"Keeping a final untouched test set"}]},{"id":"from-notebook-to-production","title":"From Notebook to Production","description":"Ship a model so other programs can use it.","lessons":[{"id":"saving-and-loading-models-with-joblib","title":"Saving and loading models with joblib"},{"id":"serving-predictions-from-a-small-web-api","title":"Serving predictions from a small web API"},{"id":"validating-inputs-at-prediction-time","title":"Validating inputs at prediction time"},{"id":"monitoring-models-and-data-drift","title":"Monitoring models and data drift"},{"id":"documenting-a-model-intended-use-and-limitations","title":"Documenting a model: intended use and limitations"}]},{"id":"capstone-project","title":"Capstone Project","description":"A complete ML project, evaluated like a professional would.","lessons":[{"id":"framing-the-problem-and-choosing-a-metric","title":"Framing the problem and choosing a metric"},{"id":"exploring-and-cleaning-the-data","title":"Exploring and cleaning the data"},{"id":"baseline-then-better-models","title":"Baseline, then better models"},{"id":"tuning-with-cross-validation","title":"Tuning with cross-validation"},{"id":"final-evaluation-on-the-test-set","title":"Final evaluation on the test set"},{"id":"writing-up-results-and-limitations","title":"Writing up results and limitations"}]}]}'::jsonb),
    ('deep-learning', 390, '{"title":"Deep Learning with PyTorch","tagline":"Build, train, and debug neural networks, from tensors to a tiny transformer.","level":"Intermediate → Advanced (Python + basic ML)","hours":40,"accent":"#EE4C2C","badge":"DL","requires":["python"],"category":"ai","stack":"Python","tutorGuidelines":["Before importing a library (numpy, pandas, scikit-learn, torch, etc.), check it is installed with python3 -c \"import <name>; print(<name>.__version__)\". If it is missing, ask the learner before installing, and prefer a virtual environment (python3 -m venv .venv).","ML library APIs change between versions. Check the installed version and use APIs that exist in it; if unsure whether a function or argument exists, test it in the terminal first.","Never make up accuracy scores, loss values, or training results — run the code and report the real numbers. Explain that results vary with random seeds, and set seeds when reproducibility matters.","Do not invent facts about specific AI models, companies, release dates, parameter counts, or benchmark leaderboards. Your knowledge may be outdated — say so and teach the underlying concepts instead.","Keep examples small enough to run quickly on a laptop CPU (small datasets such as the ones built into scikit-learn, few epochs). Say when a real project would need more data or a GPU.","Be honest about limitations: models can be wrong, biased, or overfit. Teach evaluation on held-out data and never present a model output as guaranteed truth.","Check for PyTorch with python3 -c \"import torch; print(torch.__version__, torch.cuda.is_available())\". Assume CPU unless that says a GPU is available.","Datasets and pretrained weights (torchvision) are downloaded from the internet. Tell the learner roughly what will be downloaded before running such code.","Print tensor shapes often. Most deep learning bugs are shape bugs, so teach the learner to check shapes themselves."],"buildsOn":"python","modules":[{"id":"tensors-